/-!
# Restriction of scalars and derived completeness on a ringed site

Let `𝒞` be a site, let `𝒪 ⟶ 𝒪'` be a homomorphism of sheaves of rings and let `ℐ ⊂ 𝒪` be a
sheaf of ideals. [Stacks, Tag 099C] asserts that the inverse image of `D_comp(𝒪, ℐ)` under the
restriction functor `D(𝒪') ⟶ D(𝒪)` is `D_comp(𝒪', ℐ𝒪')`. This file constructs the sheaf of
ideals `ℐ𝒪'`, proves the compatibilities on which the source's proof rests, shows that
restriction of scalars commutes with the countable products of the derived categories, and
proves one of the two inclusions: a complex derived complete along `ℐ𝒪'` restricts to one
derived complete along `ℐ`.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.extendIdeal`: the sheaf of ideals `ℐ𝒪'` of `𝒪'` generated by the
  image of a sheaf of ideals `ℐ` of `𝒪`.
* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.DerivedRestrictScalars.pushforwardTopos_coefficientChange`: the
  pushforward of sheaves of modules along a change of coefficient rings, the morphism of ringed
  topoi `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedToposSiteHom.coefficientChange` over the identity of the site,
  is restriction of scalars.

## Main results

* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.DerivedRestrictScalars.isDerivedComplete_restrictScalars_of_extendIdeal`:
  the inclusion `D_comp(𝒪', ℐ𝒪') ⊆` the inverse image of `D_comp(𝒪, ℐ)`, which is the half of
  [Stacks, Tag 099C] proved here.
* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.DerivedRestrictScalars.preservesProducts_mapDerivedCategory`: the
  functor `D(𝒪') ⟶ D(𝒪)` induced by restriction of scalars preserves countable products.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.mem_extendIdeal_of_mem` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.extendIdeal_le`: `ℐ𝒪'` contains the image of `ℐ`, and is the
  smallest sheaf of ideals of `𝒪'` that does.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.restrictScalars_comp_overFunctor`: restriction of scalars along
  `𝒪 ⟶ 𝒪'` commutes with restriction to the slice site over an object, the two composites being
  equal functors.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.restrictScalars_map_globalSectionToEnd` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.restrictScalars_map_terminalSectionToEnd`: restriction of scalars
  takes multiplication by the image in `𝒪'` of a section of `𝒪` to multiplication by that
  section, which is the source's observation that it does not matter whether `f` is read as a
  section of `𝒪` or of `𝒪'`.
* `isDerivedCompleteAt_map_of_smul_id`: an additive functor which preserves countable products
  and which matches two scalars preserves derived completeness at them.
* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.DerivedRestrictScalars.mapDerivedCategory_map_smul_id`: a scalar match
  which holds degreewise on cochain complexes holds in the derived category.

## Implementation notes

`ℐ𝒪'` is the infimum of the sheaves of ideals of `𝒪'` through which the image of `ℐ` factors;
being an infimum it is computed section by section, which is what makes
`mem_extendIdeal_of_mem` available.

A section of `𝒪` over `U` and the corresponding section of `𝒪` viewed as a sheaf of modules over
itself have definitionally equal types but are not syntactically interchangeable, so a membership
assertion written for one of them is not rewritable by lemmas stated for the other. The
sectionwise description of an infimum is therefore used in `mem_extendIdeal_of_mem` through an
auxiliary claim about a section of the sheaf of modules, where the rewriting is available, which
is then applied to a section of the structure sheaf.

The commutation `restrictScalars_comp_overFunctor` is the statement for an arbitrary site of
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.QcohOverAlgebra.restrictScalars_comp_overFunctor`, which is the same
assertion for the site of opens of a scheme.

Multiplication by a section is the natural endomorphism of the identity functor of
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.terminalSectionToEnd`, in the spelling in which the derived-completeness
predicate `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.IsDerivedComplete` of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/FormalGeometry/DerivedCompletionRinged/Basic.lean` consumes it.
Restriction of scalars leaves the underlying sheaf of abelian groups and the underlying maps
unchanged, so the comparison of the two multiplications is the naturality of `𝒪 ⟶ 𝒪'`.

### Countable products, and why no exactness of products is needed

Tag 0999 tests derived completeness of `K` at a section `f ∈ ℐ(U)` by the invertibility of
`oneSubSmulShift (K|_U) f`, an endomorphism of the countable product of copies of `K|_U` *in the
derived category* `D(𝒪_U)`, so transporting that condition along restriction of scalars needs
`PreservesLimitsOfShape (Discrete ℕ) (SheafOfModules.restrictScalars α).mapDerivedCategory`.
The route taken for the affine analogue [Stacks, Tag 0924] in
`StacksFrontier/Homology/DerivedComplete/CompleteRestrictScalars.lean` is unavailable here: it rests
on `HasExactLimitsOfShape (Discrete ℕ)`, Grothendieck's axiom AB4*, which fails for sheaves of
modules — a countable product of epimorphisms of sheaves need not be an epimorphism, which is
also why the countable products of `D(𝒪)` come from K-injective resolutions
(`Stacks.KInjectiveProducts.hasProductsOfShape_derivedCategory_sheafOfModules`) rather than
termwise.

`preservesProducts_mapDerivedCategory` obtains the instance with no
exactness hypothesis, by adjunction instead. Restriction of scalars along `𝒪 ⟶ 𝒪'` is the
pushforward of sheaves of modules along the morphism of ringed topoi `coefficientChange` over
the identity
of the site, the derived pullback `Lf^*` of [Stacks, Tag 06YY] exists for every morphism of
ringed topoi, and `Lf^* ⊣ Rf_*` is [Stacks, Tag 07A6]; restriction of scalars being exact, the
functor it induces on derived categories is a right derived functor of the degreewise pushforward
and hence isomorphic to `Rf_*`, and a right adjoint preserves limits.

That route constrains the universes. `Functor.IsMorphismOfSites` is available only for small
categories in a single universe, so the results below are stated for a site `𝒞 : Type u` with
`Category.{u} 𝒞` and `RingCat.{u}`-valued sheaves — that is for `RingedSite.{u, u, u}` and
sheaves of modules in universe `u`, which is the specialization at `v = w = u` of the generality
of `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.IsDerivedComplete`. The slice sites inherit the hypotheses:
`Over U` is small over the same universe, and the restriction of `𝒪 ⟶ 𝒪'` to it is the image
under `sheafCompose` of a homomorphism of sheaves of commutative rings on it.

The derived-completeness predicate is defined at `HasDerivedCategory.standard`, which that file
installs as a file-level local instance, so the model is baked into the predicate's type; this
file installs it as a local instance for the same reason. `mapDerivedCategoryCompIso` is restated
here rather than imported, because every copy of it in this repository —
`CategoryTheory.Functor.mapDerivedCategoryCompIso` among them — lives in a module about schemes
or about a particular site, downstream of this one. The results about the morphism of
ringed topoi are stated in the namespace `Stacks.SheafOfModules.DerivedRestrictScalars` rather
than `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite`, because they are about sheaves of modules on a site and a
ringed topos rather than about a ringed site.

### The other half of Tag 099C

The attribute below is in phrase form: this file proves only the inclusion
`D_comp(𝒪', ℐ𝒪') ⊆` the inverse image of `D_comp(𝒪, ℐ)`. The reverse inclusion, and with it the
inverse-image equality that the tag asserts, is
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.DerivedRestrictScalars.isDerivedComplete_restrictScalars_iff` of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/FormalGeometry/DerivedCompletionRinged/RestrictScalarsConverse.lean`, which is downstream of this file
because it also needs [Stacks, Tag 0998].

A section of `ℐ𝒪'` over `U` is not in general the image of a section of `ℐ`: `extendIdeal` is the
*smallest* sheaf of ideals of `𝒪'` containing that image. So the reverse inclusion asks for
`T(E|_U, f') = 0` for every `f' ∈ (ℐ𝒪')(U)` knowing it for the images of sections of `ℐ`, and by
`extendIdeal_le` that reduces to the sections at which `E` is derived complete forming a sheaf of
ideals in `𝒪'`. That is exactly [Stacks, Tag 0998], formalized for a `RingedSite.{u, u, u}` as
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedCompleteIdealSheaf` of
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/FormalGeometry/DerivedCompletionRinged/VanishingIdealSheafCondition.lean`. The affine analogue
[Stacks, Tag 0924] uses the corresponding affine statement [Stacks, Tag 091Q] at the same point
of its proof. Transporting `T(E|_U, f) = 0` from `𝒪` back up to `T(E|_U, f') = 0` over `𝒪'`
additionally reads `isDerivedCompleteAt_map_of_smul_id` backwards, so it asks for restriction of
scalars on derived categories to *reflect* invertibility, which is why the reverse inclusion is
not simply the argument below run in the other direction.

## References

